    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Bangkok began as a customs outpost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river">Chao Phraya River</a>, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a>'s capital of Thonburi in 1767, and was refounded on the east bank by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> in 1782.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a> the city took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press, paved roads, railways, electricity and Western-style architecture, and Chinese immigrants led its trade.</li><li>In the 20th century Bangkok was the stage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>'s political struggles, from the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> in 1932 to student uprisings and coups, while its population grew from about 50,000 in 1822 to millions.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Bangkok's full ceremonial name, given in the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a>, is listed in Guinness World Records as the world's longest place name, at 168 letters.</p></aside>
    </section>

