    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">15th century</span><span class="rf-text">Bangkok is a customs outpost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> on the Chao Phraya</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1538 or 1542</span><span class="rf-text">A waterway is dug across a loop of the river, dividing the town and making its western part an island</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1688</span><span class="rf-text">Siamese troops besiege the French in Bangkok's eastern fort for four months before the French withdraw</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1767</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a> makes Thonburi his capital after the Burmese destroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1782</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> moves the capital to the east bank and erects the city pillar on 21 April</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1893</span><span class="rf-text">French gunboats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> Bangkok in the Paknam Incident</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1932</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy</a> is abolished</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1971 to 1972</span><span class="rf-text">The provinces of Phra Nakhon and Thonburi are merged to form the present city</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wat-arun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-arun.jpg" alt="Wat Arun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wat-arun">Wat Arun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%932.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mastertongapollo, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bangkok stands in the delta of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river">Chao Phraya River</a> in the central plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. The area is flat and low-lying, with an average elevation of 1.5 m, and most of it was originally swampland, drained and irrigated for agriculture by canals (khlong) dug between the 16th and 19th centuries. It is not known exactly when the area was first settled. It probably began as a small farming and trading community in a meander of the river, within the mandala of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>'s influence, and by the 15th century it was an important customs outpost: a document from the reign of the Ayutthayan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommarachathirat-ii">Chao Sam Phraya</a> (1424 to 1448) gives the title of its customs official as Nai Phra Khanon Thonburi.</p><p>At that time the river flowed through what are now the Bangkok Noi and Bangkok Yai canals, forming a large loop in which the town lay. In the reign of King Chairacha, in 1538 or 1542, a waterway was excavated to bypass the loop and shorten the route for ships sailing up to Ayutthaya. The river came to follow the new channel, which divided the town and made its western part an island. This may have given the town the name Bang Ko, 'island village', later Bangkok. Another theory derives it from Bang Makok, after a plant with olive-like fruit, which would fit the former name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-arun">Wat Arun</a>, Wat Makok. The origin of the name is unclear. The royal chronicles of the reign of King Maha Chakkraphat (1548 to 1568) first mention the town, as Thonburi Si Mahasamut, and Bangkok was probably a colloquial name that foreign visitors adopted widely.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Bangkok&#8221; cites where it covers Name:</p><ul><li>Marc Askew, Bangkok: Place, Practice and Representation (Routledge, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mongkut"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongkut.jpg" alt="Mongkut" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_King_of_Siam_MET_DP-573-001_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Thomson, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The importance of Bangkok rose with the maritime trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, which was at its height under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narai">Narai</a> (1656 to 1688). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> records noted that passing ships had to declare their goods and passengers and pay customs duties, and that their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> were held there before they could go upriver. Around 1685 to 1687 a fort of Western design was built on the east side of the river under the French engineer de la Mare.</p><p>After it became the capital in 1782, Bangkok grew only slightly through the 18th and early 19th centuries; the British diplomat John Crawfurd, visiting in 1822, estimated its population at no more than 50,000. Its economy expanded through international trade, first with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and then with Western merchants who returned in the early to mid-19th century. Under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a> (reigned 1851 to 1868) the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engine</a>, modern shipbuilding and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press came to the city, and Charoen Krung Road, its first paved street, was built in 1862 to 1864. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a> (1868 to 1910) more canals and roads were built, and railway and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> services, electricity and electric trams were introduced. He commissioned the neoclassical Ananta Samakhom Throne Hall at the new Dusit Palace, linked to the old centre by Ratchadamnoen Avenue.</p><p>Chulalongkorn University was founded in 1916. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the city grew rapidly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> aid and government-sponsored investment, and its population rose from 1.8 million to 3 million in the 1960s. Rapid growth continued through the 1980s and early 1990s until the Asian financial crisis of 1997.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Bangkok&#8221; cites where it covers Demography, Economy, Calls to move the capital:</p><ul><li>Marc Askew, Bangkok: Place, Practice and Representation (Routledge, 2004)</li><li>Sidney Goldstein, The Demography of Bangkok: A case study of differentials between big city and rural populations (Institute of Population Studies, Chulalongkorn University, 1972)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grand-palace.jpg" alt="Grand Palace" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0005574_-_Wat_Phra_Kaew_006.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Preecha.MJ, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bangkok was held by the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> until the Burmese razed that city in 1767. Phraya Tak, a general who had fought in Ayutthaya's defence, recovered Ayutthaya and Bangkok, declared himself king on 28 December 1767 (he is known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a>) and established Thonburi as his capital, on the west bank. Reasons given include the totality of Ayutthaya's destruction and Thonburi's strategic location; the old Chinese trading settlement on the east bank also let Taksin use his Chinese connections to import <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and revive trade.</p><p>In 1782 a coup was mounted against Taksin and the general Chao Phraya Chakri made himself king, later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Phutthayotfa Chulalok</a> or Rama I. He moved the capital to the east bank, built fortifications and a ring of moats round Rattanakosin Island, and erected the city pillar on 21 April 1782, which is regarded as the formal date of the city's establishment. He modelled the city on Ayutthaya, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-palace">Front Palace</a> and royal temples by the river beside the royal field, now Sanam Luang. Treaties of 1826 and 1833 name the capital the City of Sia-Yut'hia, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a> gave it its full ceremonial name.</p><p>The monthon Krung Thep Phra Mahanakhon was set up for the capital under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy</a> was abolished in 1932. Bangkok was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and after it British and Indian troops landed in September 1945 and disarmed the Japanese. Phra Nakhon and Thonburi were merged in 1971 to 1972 to form the present city, whose governor has been elected since 1985.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the city's foundation most people lived by the river or the canals, often in floating houses, and waterways were the main means of transport; in the 19th century the city was known as the "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> of the East". Land transport later surpassed the canals in importance, and people moved from floating dwellings to permanent buildings. Settlements sprawled along both banks outside the walls. Forced settlers, mostly captives of war, formed several ethnic communities there, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>, northern Thai, Lao, Vietnamese, Mon and Malay.</p><p>The Chinese were the most prominent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> moved the Chinese already settled on the east bank to the area between Wat Sam Pluem and Wat Sampheng, which developed into Chinatown, and by 1835 Sampheng had become a bustling market. Large numbers of Chinese immigrants settled in the early 19th century, and Europeans visiting in the 1820s estimated that they formed over half of the city's population; other estimates give up to three-fourths in 1828 and almost half in the 1950s. They excelled in trade and led the development of a market economy. Chinese immigration was restricted from the 1930s and effectively ceased after 1949.</p><p>Other quarters grew up round particular communities: the Indian community at Phahurat, where the Gurdwara Siri Guru Singh Sabha was founded in 1933; Ban Khrua, home to descendants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> who settled in the late 18th century; and the Old Farang Quarter, where European diplomats and merchants lived. According to the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, 93 per cent of the city's people were Buddhist.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/phetracha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phetracha.jpg" alt="Phetracha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/phetracha">Phetracha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PITERA_TJAY_Rex_Siam_by_Gaspar_Bouttat_1690.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gaspar Bouttats, (sr.), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1687 the French general Desfarges, who had arrived with the second French embassy, secured King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narai">Narai</a>'s permission to board troops in Bangkok's fort. This caused resentment among Siamese nobles led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phetracha">Phetracha</a>, and in the Siamese revolution of 1688 Narai was overthrown and 40,000 Siamese troops besieged the eastern fort for four months, until an agreement allowed the French to withdraw. The revolution virtually severed Siam's ties with the West and steered its trade towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and Phetracha ordered the eastern fort demolished.</p><p>In 1893 the French sent gunboats up the Chao Phraya to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> Bangkok in the Paknam Incident, and Siam conceded territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> Bangkok was occupied by Japan and subjected to Allied bombing.</p><p>The city was also the scene of the country's political upheavals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy</a> was abolished in 1932. There were student uprisings in 1973 and 1976, anti-military demonstrations in 1992, and street protests from 2006, including those by groups opposing and supporting the former prime minister Thaksin Shinawatra from 2006 to 2013, followed by a coup in 2014 and a student-led movement in 2020.</p><p>Floods and the ground itself also troubled the city. Subsidence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> pumping, first found in the 1970s, reached 120 mm a year in 1981, and parts of the city now lie 1 m below sea level. Severe flooding affected much of the city in 1995 and 2011, when most of the northern, eastern and western districts were flooded, in places for over two months. The financial crisis of 1997 stalled the city's growth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn.jpg" alt="Chulalongkorn" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chulalongkorn_LoC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bain News Service, publisher., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bangkok's name has several layers. Foreigners called the city Bangkok throughout, while Thai sources name the capital Rattanakosin, after the kingdom of the period. Krung Thep and Krung Thep Maha Nakhon, shortened forms of the ceremonial name, came into use near the end of the 19th century. That full name, composed of Pali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> roots, is commonly translated 'City of Angels'. The year of the city pillar's erection later became the start of the Rattanakosin Era, after calendar reforms by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Rama V</a> in 1888.</p><p>The historic centre remains Rattanakosin Island in Phra Nakhon District, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a> and the City Pillar Shrine, the primary symbols of the city's founding, and important Buddhist temples. The neoclassical buildings of the Dusit Palace and Ratchadamnoen Avenue show the European influence of Chulalongkorn's reign. Phu Khao Thong, the only hill in the metropolitan area, began as a very large chedi built at Wat Saket by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a>, which collapsed during construction because the soft soil could not support its weight; over the next decades the abandoned mound took the shape of a natural hill.</p><p>Bangkok is today the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, with an estimated 9.1 million people, and all branches of the national government sit there. It grew into an extreme primate city: in 1980 its population was fifty-one times that of Hat Yai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songkhla">Songkhla</a>, then the second-largest urban centre. It covers 1,568.7 square kilometres and is governed by the Bangkok Metropolitan Administration.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Bangkok grew from a customs outpost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river">Chao Phraya River</a> into the capital of Thonburi in 1767 and of Rattanakosin in 1782. It was the centre of Siam's modernisation and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>'s political struggles, and is today the country's capital and largest city.</em></p>

